pc, re my suggestion of "dei":
> Not bad.  It is strictly the sentence uttered at here-and-now, not 
> here-and-now itself (as 20:16 CDT is the time of h-n-n, not what it 
> is the time of). But it is the most visible/audible thing to hook 
> deixis on -- for deixis does need a stable hook.  Just as a tense 
> system does.  And without either a name for h-n-n or the natural rule 
> of Lojban tenses, the predicates system for tenses (et al) won't work 
> (somebody's paper on somebody else's paper in the early 1960's -- 
> probably Barbara Partee and maybe James McCawley in that order).  

I take the view that the here-and-now is defined on the basis of
"dei", so "here" = "place of dei" and "now" = "time of dei".

